3 Final remarks
The probability of success can be improved by run-
ning the algorithm c times. Let y be such that T[y] is
the minimum among the outcomes. With probabil-
ity at least 1 − 1/2c
, T[y] holds the minimum of the
table. Clearly, the probability of success is even bet-
ter if we run the algorithm only once with time-out
c2m0, because then we use the information provided
by the previous steps.
If the values in T are not distinct, we use the
same algorithm as for the simplified case. The anal-
ysis of the general case is unchanged, except that in
lemma 1, the equation p(t,r) = 1/r now becomes an
inequality, p(t,r) ≤ 1/r. Hence, the lower bound for
the success probability given in theorem 1 for the
simplified case is also a lower bound for the general
case.
